  "query_preview": "..Following the historic rainfall over the weekend, \na much drier airmass will remain dominant over northern Arizona \nthrough the week. Even so, use caution when driving as many low \nwater crossings will still be flooded or otherwise inundated with \nwater. Flooding impacts may linger for a few more days.\n\nA brief ridge will move overhead today, bringing light winds and \nslightly warmer afternoon temperatures. Daytime highs will still \nfall a few degrees below early November normals for most. Some \npatchy fog will also be possible through the early morning hours \ntoday. \n\nA quick moving and dry trough will brush to the north of our area\non Wednesday. The primary impact will be some slightly breezier \nwesterly winds gusting up to 25 mph along the higher terrain, \nperhaps as high as 35 mph along/north of the White Mountains. \n\nLighter winds are expected by Thursday into the weekend, however \nconditions will become ideal for breezy and localized northeast \nwinds to develop downwind of terrain features each night. Another \npassing shortwave on Friday may bring a slight chance of light \nshowers to the White Mountains, then continued dry conditions are \nexpected into the weekend.",
